<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominique de Gourgues</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1530–1593</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dominique de Gourgues was a French soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. In 1568, to avenge the Spanish massacre of the Huguenot colonists of Fort Caroline in 1565, he led a privately financed expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, where, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a> allies, he captured the Spanish forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St Johns River</a> and hanged their garrisons. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> without founding a settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dominique-de-gourgues.jpg" alt="Dominique de Gourgues" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominique_de_Gourgues.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
